    84	
    85	This is an identity in the ramified extension, with derivation
    86	`d/dt=(1/((k+1)gamma^k))*d/dgamma`. The functions lie in
    87	`K[t^(1/(k+1)),pi]`, generally NOT in `K[t,pi]`. They may be embedded
    88	in GGV1's `L^(k+1)`; that licenses the Laurent theorem, not its
    89	ordinary polynomial clause. For a direction satisfying that theorem's
    90	positivity hypotheses, it provides E in `L^(k+1)`. Its pullback lies
    91	in `K[gamma,gamma^-1,pi]` and obeys
    92	
    93	```text
    94	[E_pull,P]_(gamma,pi)=(k+1)gamma^k ell(P),
    95	```
    96	
    97	with the corresponding transported leading form. For original weight
    98	`(rho,sigma)`, the transformed direction is proportional to
    99	`((k+1)rho,sigma)`, and the pulled Euler weight is
   100	`(k+1)rho+sigma`, not `rho+sigma`. The exceptional endpoint `(1,1)`
   101	likewise pulls back to `(k+1,1)`.
   102	
   103	Negative gamma powers are not excluded by the theorem; multiplying E
   104	by a power to clear them is not harmless, since
   105	`[gamma^N E,P]=gamma^N[E,P]+N gamma^(N-1)E P_pi`.
   106	Even positive fractional exponents in t invalidate the old
   107	integer-polynomial support enumeration. No polynomiality assertion
   108	for the corrected Euler element is proved in this pass.
   109	
    88	Both roots d are retained, including both rational factors when
    89	`m=3s^2`. No `lambda=1` or fixed original leader is imposed.
    90	
    91	## 3. Why the global Pell/degree lever does not materialize
    92	
    93	The proposed norm equation is
    94	
    95	```text
    96	3U^2-2V^2 = T_V := 1+4x+2x(V-1)V'.                     (P)
    97	```
    98	
    99	For a degree-q polynomial V with leader v!=0, `deg T_V=2q` and its
   100	leader is `2q v^2!=0`. Thus (P) is not a small-remainder polynomial
   101	approximation: the derivative term has exactly the full square degree.
   102	At infinity it gives only `3(lc U)^2=2(q+1)v^2`. Since
   103	`lc U/v=1/(4p)-1=2d`, this is exactly `3d^2=m`, already imposed.
   104	
   105	Even subtracting that leading balance yields only
   106	
   107	```text
   108	3U^2-2(q+1)V^2
   109	 = 1+4x-2xV'+2V(xV'-qV),       degree at most 2q-1.
   110	```
   111	
   112	The `2V(xV'-qV)` term retains the unknown lower coefficients and the
   113	usual degree `2q-1` of a product of degrees q and q-1. No strict extra
   114	degree drop has been proved. No Davenport bound is invoked without a
   115	small-remainder hypothesis or with a different meaning of remainder.
   116	
   117	Nor is the left side a source of nonconstant polynomial Pell units:
   118	over k, `3a^2-2b^2=1` factors as
   119	`(sqrt(3)a-sqrt(2)b)(sqrt(3)a+sqrt(2)b)=1` in k[x]. Each factor is a
   120	unit, hence constant, so a,b are constant. The coefficient is constant,
   121	not a nonsquare polynomial defining the usual nontrivial Pell cover;
   122	and `T_V` itself changes with the unknown V. This does not rule out
   123	other transformations, but supplies no Pell-powered degree descent.
   124	
   125	The origin marking also diagnoses the most obvious branch switch
   126	exactly. Holding V fixed and replacing U by -U preserves (P), but a
   127	new A would have to satisfy
   128	
   129	```text
   130	A_tilde^2 = 2(V-U)/x^3 = 4V/x^3-A^2.                   (SW)
   131	```
   132	
   133	Because `V(0)=-1` and A is polynomial, the right side has a pole of
   134	order exactly 3 at zero, with leading term `-4/x^3`. A rational square
   135	has even valuation at every place. Thus **no rational A_tilde exists**.
   136	The switch cannot act on the marked polynomial category, let alone
   137	lower m. This is a negative control against using the other square
   138	root as a second polynomial solution; it is not nonexistence of the
   139	original A. Equivalently, for fixed V at most one U-sign can lift to
   140	a rational A, up to A's own sign.
   141	
   142	Both the origin and infinity information have therefore been used,
   143	without producing a contradiction: the origin prevents the candidate
   144	descent, while infinity returns the known leading equation. A new
   145	global termination theorem would still have to forbid simultaneous
   146	polynomiality of the two linked squares. Merely requiring those
   147	squares to terminate is the original equation under (ID).

