+### Card 2 — restricted pole removal for monomial-J Euler elements
+
+**NEW bounded mechanism within the searched history; external-theorem dependency, no receiver exclusion yet.** Suppose `P,Q in K[gamma,pi]`, over a characteristic-zero field, `[P,Q]=c gamma^k`, c≠0, integer k≥0. For a primitive integer direction(rho,sigma) with rho,sigma>0, put R=ell_(rho,sigma)(P). Correct constantization uses `t=gamma^(k+1)`, not inversion. GGV1 Theorem2.6 in `K[t^(±1/(k+1)),pi]`, followed by pullback and scalar division, supplies
+
+```
+[E,R]=gamma^k R,
+weight(E)=(k+1)rho+sigma,
+E in K[gamma^(±1),pi],   exceptional endpoint=(k+1,1).
+```
+
+References: `monomial-jacobian-euler-interface-astra-20260906.md:77–108`; primary `core-ggv-layout.txt:366–392`. The theorem's ordinary polynomial clause is not imported.
+
+**Pole-removal lemma.** Write `R=gamma^s r(pi)+...`, s≥0, deg r>0. If E had least gamma exponent u<0 with coefficient e(pi), the bracket's coefficient at exponent u+s−1 would be `u e r'−s e' r`. Its leading pi coefficient contains `u deg r−s deg e<0`, so it is nonzero. This contradicts the right side's order k+s. Hence E is polynomial under this additional hypothesis.
